1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,280
 - So now we're going to talk about loop idioms.

2
00:00:02,280 --> 00:00:06,040
 And loop idioms are patterns that have to do

3
00:00:06,040 --> 00:00:07,740
 with how we construct loops.

4
00:00:07,740 --> 00:00:11,360
 We have the mechanics of fours and whiles,

5
00:00:11,360 --> 00:00:13,880
 but ultimately we want to get something done.

6
00:00:13,880 --> 00:00:16,220
 We want to solve a problem with a loop.

7
00:00:16,220 --> 00:00:19,780
 And often what we have to do is if we have a set of things,

8
00:00:19,780 --> 00:00:22,980
 whether it's lines or strings or characters or numbers,

9
00:00:22,980 --> 00:00:25,480
 we're looking for something like the largest or the smallest

10
00:00:25,480 --> 00:00:28,260
 or we want to add them up or something like that.

11
00:00:28,260 --> 00:00:31,080
 And so we can't just say add them up.

12
00:00:31,080 --> 00:00:33,460
 We have to say go through each one

13
00:00:33,460 --> 00:00:34,880
 and do something to each one

14
00:00:34,880 --> 00:00:37,200
 and somehow achieve adding them up.

15
00:00:37,200 --> 00:00:39,280
 And the pattern that we're going to follow is

16
00:00:39,280 --> 00:00:41,860
 we're going to have this loop that's going to do all,

17
00:00:41,860 --> 00:00:46,740
 run once for each thing, right, in some chunk of data.

18
00:00:46,740 --> 00:00:48,900
 And then, but we're going to set something at the beginning

19
00:00:48,900 --> 00:00:50,760
 and then we're going to do something to each one

20
00:00:50,760 --> 00:00:52,920
 and at the end we're going to kind of get the payoff.

21
00:00:52,920 --> 00:00:54,480
 We're going to get the result.

22
00:00:54,480 --> 00:00:57,900
 So if we're doing sort of summing things,

23
00:00:57,900 --> 00:00:59,500
 we're going to have a running total.

24
00:00:59,500 --> 00:01:02,320
 And so this will be like t equals zero.

25
00:01:02,320 --> 00:01:06,580
 And then this will be t equals t plus the thing value.

26
00:01:06,580 --> 00:01:08,620
 And then, but this is not the real total,

27
00:01:08,620 --> 00:01:10,760
 it's the running total during the loop.

28
00:01:10,760 --> 00:01:13,720
 But at the end it is the real total.

29
00:01:13,720 --> 00:01:15,760
 And so we're going to look at what you do

30
00:01:15,760 --> 00:01:17,900
 before the loop starts, during the loop,

31
00:01:17,900 --> 00:01:20,360
 and then what you get after the loop

32
00:01:20,360 --> 00:01:22,860
 and how you can use that.

33
00:01:22,860 --> 00:01:24,440
 So we're going to use this loop.

34
00:01:24,440 --> 00:01:26,660
 It's just going to loop through a set of six numbers

35
00:01:26,660 --> 00:01:29,100
 over and over and over again, right.

36
00:01:29,100 --> 00:01:30,700
 So we're going to do something before the loop,

37
00:01:30,700 --> 00:01:31,940
 we're going to do something after the loop,

38
00:01:31,940 --> 00:01:34,340
 and then we're going to run the loop some number of times.

39
00:01:34,340 --> 00:01:36,820
 And in this case, thing is our iteration variable

40
00:01:36,820 --> 00:01:40,220
 because I'm using un-pneumonic variables now.

41
00:01:40,220 --> 00:01:45,180
 So it's going to run nine, 41, 12, three, 74, and 15.

42
00:01:45,180 --> 00:01:47,060
 So it's going to run and print these things out.

43
00:01:47,060 --> 00:01:49,660
 So it runs this loop six times and away we go.

44
00:01:49,660 --> 00:01:52,440
 Now this loop does nothing except print stuff out.

45
00:01:52,440 --> 00:01:53,980
 Of course, I like to do that first,

46
00:01:53,980 --> 00:01:55,860
 is always print things out.

47
00:01:55,860 --> 00:02:00,300
 To make sure that sort of my brain is functioning.

48
00:02:00,300 --> 00:02:04,300
 So, to kind of understand how these loops work,

49
00:02:04,300 --> 00:02:08,420
 I'm going to ask you to function as a program.

50
00:02:08,420 --> 00:02:11,180
 And I'm going to show you some numbers in succession,

51
00:02:11,180 --> 00:02:14,640
 and I want you to mentally figure out

52
00:02:14,640 --> 00:02:16,520
 what the largest number is, but more importantly,

53
00:02:16,520 --> 00:02:19,520
 think about how your brain is solving this problem

54
00:02:19,520 --> 00:02:20,940
 of what is the largest number,

55
00:02:20,940 --> 00:02:22,300
 given that I'm only going to show them to you

56
00:02:22,300 --> 00:02:24,140
 one at a time for a little while,

57
00:02:24,140 --> 00:02:25,780
 and your brain has to do something.

58
00:02:25,780 --> 00:02:28,140
 Now, imagine I was going to show you thousands of numbers.

59
00:02:28,140 --> 00:02:29,940
 I'm not, but imagine I was.

60
00:02:29,940 --> 00:02:32,660
 How would you organize yourself in a way

61
00:02:32,660 --> 00:02:34,420
 so that for like an hour and a half,

62
00:02:34,420 --> 00:02:36,460
 you could sit here as I showed you numbers,

63
00:02:36,460 --> 00:02:39,160
 and you keep track of the largest number

64
00:02:39,160 --> 00:02:41,680
 that you've seen of all the numbers?

65
00:02:41,680 --> 00:02:43,020
 Okay, so here we go.

66
00:02:43,020 --> 00:02:45,180
 Here's your first number.

67
00:02:45,180 --> 00:02:49,180
 Second number.

68
00:02:49,180 --> 00:02:52,060
 Third number.

69
00:02:52,060 --> 00:02:55,040
 Fourth number.

70
00:02:56,180 --> 00:02:57,260
 Fifth number.

71
00:02:57,260 --> 00:03:01,140
 Sixth and last number.

72
00:03:01,140 --> 00:03:03,560
 What was the largest number?

73
00:03:03,560 --> 00:03:08,260
 What was it?

74
00:03:08,260 --> 00:03:11,220
 Well, it wasn't too hard.

75
00:03:11,220 --> 00:03:15,040
 It was 74, but that's not the question.

76
00:03:15,040 --> 00:03:17,700
 How did your brain arrive at 74?

77
00:03:17,700 --> 00:03:20,800
 So here's all the numbers.

78
00:03:20,800 --> 00:03:22,260
 If I was showing you all the numbers

79
00:03:22,260 --> 00:03:25,660
 and asked you what's the largest number,

80
00:03:25,660 --> 00:03:29,300
 your eyes would've sort of gone (growls)

81
00:03:29,300 --> 00:03:30,940
 and then you got to 74.

82
00:03:30,940 --> 00:03:34,020
 And you wouldn't do it in any particular order.

83
00:03:34,020 --> 00:03:36,260
 Your eyes would just like see the 74

84
00:03:36,260 --> 00:03:38,660
 and it would just throw smaller numbers away,

85
00:03:38,660 --> 00:03:41,100
 and it would move really quickly to what the answer is.

86
00:03:41,100 --> 00:03:44,420
 Even if there was several hundred numbers on the screen,

87
00:03:44,420 --> 00:03:47,420
 your mind would sort of move fluidly

88
00:03:47,420 --> 00:03:50,320
 wherever it felt like moving and then arrive at it.

89
00:03:50,320 --> 00:03:52,120
 And probably what it would do is,

90
00:03:52,120 --> 00:03:55,220
 it would do something like kind of move like this,

91
00:03:55,220 --> 00:03:58,060
 find this and then sort of check to make sure that it's okay

92
00:03:58,060 --> 00:04:02,180
 and then say like, "Okay, I got 74, I'm done."

93
00:04:02,180 --> 00:04:03,580
 That's not how computers do it.

94
00:04:03,580 --> 00:04:05,540
 That is not how computers do it.

95
00:04:05,540 --> 00:04:07,460
 They do not move fluidly.

96
00:04:07,460 --> 00:04:09,900
 But they are highly dedicated.

97
00:04:09,900 --> 00:04:14,860
 They're going to do something (imitates buzzer)

98
00:04:14,860 --> 00:04:15,700
 74.

99
00:04:15,700 --> 00:04:19,580
 But how would you construct a loop to achieve this?

100
00:04:19,580 --> 00:04:21,700
 So let's take a look.

101
00:04:21,700 --> 00:04:24,260
 You could create a variable called largest so far.

102
00:04:24,260 --> 00:04:26,020
 And this is the largest variable,

103
00:04:26,020 --> 00:04:27,980
 the value that you've seen in the list so far.

104
00:04:27,980 --> 00:04:29,620
 Now I haven't shown you any numbers yet,

105
00:04:29,620 --> 00:04:32,520
 so we'll just set this to negative one to get us started.

106
00:04:32,520 --> 00:04:35,320
 So now, we see three.

107
00:04:35,320 --> 00:04:37,280
 And we're like, "Oh, that's better than negative one."

108
00:04:37,280 --> 00:04:38,120
 It's our first number,

109
00:04:38,120 --> 00:04:40,740
 so it's probably the largest we've seen so far, right?

110
00:04:40,740 --> 00:04:42,300
 Great, 41.

111
00:04:42,300 --> 00:04:44,340
 Oh, that's bigger than the largest we've seen so far,

112
00:04:44,340 --> 00:04:45,340
 so we'll keep it.

113
00:04:45,340 --> 00:04:48,460
 12 is not bigger than 41, so we're not going to keep it.

114
00:04:48,460 --> 00:04:50,460
 Notice this keeping thing.

115
00:04:50,460 --> 00:04:51,900
 Nine is not bigger than 41,

116
00:04:51,900 --> 00:04:53,300
 so there's no point to keeping it.

117
00:04:53,300 --> 00:04:56,060
 74 is bigger than 41, so we'll keep it.

118
00:04:56,060 --> 00:04:57,100
 Is this the largest number?

119
00:04:57,100 --> 00:04:57,940
 We don't know.

120
00:04:57,940 --> 00:04:59,540
 We don't know until we're done.

121
00:04:59,540 --> 00:05:01,780
 15, not better than 74.

122
00:05:01,780 --> 00:05:03,420
 So now, we're all done.

123
00:05:03,420 --> 00:05:06,060
 And hooray, hooray, hooray.

124
00:05:06,060 --> 00:05:08,220
 We have the largest number.

125
00:05:08,220 --> 00:05:10,620
 And we had this variable that we kept,

126
00:05:10,620 --> 00:05:14,260
 the largest number that we'd seen up to this point.

127
00:05:14,260 --> 00:05:17,060
 And then when we know that we're done at the end,

128
00:05:17,060 --> 00:05:19,220
 then that becomes the largest.

129
00:05:19,220 --> 00:05:22,000
 So if you look at all the numbers,

130
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:23,380
 keeping track of the largest so far,

131
00:05:23,380 --> 00:05:24,340
 at the end of all the numbers,

132
00:05:24,340 --> 00:05:27,700
 the largest so far and the largest are the same thing.

133
00:05:27,700 --> 00:05:30,580
 And so that's how you get this idea

134
00:05:30,580 --> 00:05:32,900
 of something you're doing during the loop

135
00:05:32,900 --> 00:05:34,540
 is not really the answer,

136
00:05:34,540 --> 00:05:38,440
 but by the time the loop is done, you will have the answer.

137
00:05:38,440 --> 00:05:40,540
 And so here's a bit of code that does this.

138
00:05:40,540 --> 00:05:42,140
 Use it with our numbers, right?

139
00:05:42,140 --> 00:05:44,820
 So let's take a look.

140
00:05:44,820 --> 00:05:46,560
 So I have this variable called largest so far.

141
00:05:46,560 --> 00:05:49,100
 I set it to negative one before the loop.

142
00:05:49,100 --> 00:05:51,060
 Remember, there's a loop before and a loop after

143
00:05:51,060 --> 00:05:53,420
 and a loop in the middle before it's negative one.

144
00:05:53,420 --> 00:05:57,180
 So now the num, remember underscores are okay.

145
00:05:57,180 --> 00:05:59,140
 That's my iteration variable.

146
00:05:59,140 --> 00:06:01,820
 If nine is greater than largest so far,

147
00:06:01,820 --> 00:06:03,260
 well, largest so far is negative one,

148
00:06:03,260 --> 00:06:04,980
 so that's true, so this code's going to run.

149
00:06:04,980 --> 00:06:07,420
 So we're going to remember the new number.

150
00:06:07,420 --> 00:06:08,580
 So this is nine.

151
00:06:08,580 --> 00:06:10,940
 And so nine ends up in largest so far.

152
00:06:10,940 --> 00:06:11,780
 And then print it out.

153
00:06:11,780 --> 00:06:16,380
 And so largest so far is nine after we saw the number nine.

154
00:06:16,380 --> 00:06:19,340
 Then we do it again.

155
00:06:19,340 --> 00:06:21,540
 So now 41 comes in.

156
00:06:21,540 --> 00:06:23,780
 And is 41 greater than nine?

157
00:06:23,780 --> 00:06:25,160
 The answer is yes, it is.

158
00:06:25,160 --> 00:06:26,560
 So we're going to run this code.

159
00:06:26,560 --> 00:06:31,380
 Copy 41 into largest so far and then print it out.

160
00:06:31,380 --> 00:06:36,380
 And largest so far is 41 after we saw the number 41.

161
00:06:36,380 --> 00:06:40,220
 Now we're going to run the loop again with 12, okay?

162
00:06:40,220 --> 00:06:41,780
 And you get the idea, I hope.

163
00:06:41,780 --> 00:06:43,340
 Is 12 greater than 41,

164
00:06:43,340 --> 00:06:44,920
 which is the largest we've seen so far?

165
00:06:44,920 --> 00:06:46,660
 And the answer is no, it is not.

166
00:06:46,660 --> 00:06:47,660
 So we skip.

167
00:06:47,660 --> 00:06:51,660
 So the largest so far stays 41 even though we saw 12.

168
00:06:51,660 --> 00:06:53,800
 Meaning we're sort of like ratcheting up,

169
00:06:53,800 --> 00:06:56,140
 but we never ratchet back down.

170
00:06:56,140 --> 00:07:00,500
 So we run it again with three and 41.

171
00:07:00,500 --> 00:07:02,140
 And we skip this.

172
00:07:02,140 --> 00:07:04,660
 And then the largest so far is 41,

173
00:07:04,660 --> 00:07:06,300
 even though we just saw three.

174
00:07:06,300 --> 00:07:09,120
 And now we see 74.

175
00:07:09,120 --> 00:07:11,300
 Is 74 greater than 41?

176
00:07:11,300 --> 00:07:13,400
 See, we never are looking at all the numbers.

177
00:07:13,400 --> 00:07:15,280
 We're only looking at the window on the numbers

178
00:07:15,280 --> 00:07:17,820
 of the current number that we're looking at.

179
00:07:17,820 --> 00:07:21,100
 So is 74 greater than 41?

180
00:07:21,100 --> 00:07:22,100
 The answer is yes.

181
00:07:22,100 --> 00:07:25,660
 So we run this code and then we capture the 74.

182
00:07:25,660 --> 00:07:30,180
 So we've seen, we just saw 74 and it is the largest so far.

183
00:07:30,180 --> 00:07:32,880
 And then we run it again with 15.

184
00:07:32,880 --> 00:07:36,640
 But 74 is our largest so far and so it skips.

185
00:07:36,640 --> 00:07:40,580
 So 74 remains largest so far after 15.

186
00:07:40,580 --> 00:07:43,260
 And now we're finished because we just ran the last thing.

187
00:07:43,260 --> 00:07:45,220
 The for loop takes care of everything.

188
00:07:45,220 --> 00:07:48,020
 And jumps to this print statement and says afterwards,

189
00:07:48,020 --> 00:07:49,460
 largest so far is 74.

190
00:07:49,460 --> 00:07:53,540
 But at this point, it's also the largest.

191
00:07:53,540 --> 00:07:58,540
 So largest so far became largest when our loop finished.

192
00:07:58,540 --> 00:08:00,860
 So that sort of gives you this notion

193
00:08:00,860 --> 00:08:05,700
 of how we construct something at the beginning,

194
00:08:05,700 --> 00:08:07,100
 some kind of thing that we're going to do

195
00:08:07,100 --> 00:08:08,980
 over and over and over again,

196
00:08:08,980 --> 00:08:11,120
 and then something at the end.

197
00:08:11,120 --> 00:08:12,540
 And we put some print statements in

198
00:08:12,540 --> 00:08:15,360
 just so we can watch it and see what's going on.

199
00:08:15,360 --> 00:08:17,820
 So coming up next,

200
00:08:17,820 --> 00:08:20,300
 we're going to talk about some more loop patterns.

201
00:08:20,300 --> 00:08:22,940
 Some counting, totaling, averaging,

202
00:08:22,940 --> 00:08:24,720
 and finding the smallest number.

203
